  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Urgent Water Supply Needs in Al Makha City, Reserve Allocation 9</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The International Organization for Migration (IOM) is bringing critically needed freshwater to an estimated 120,000 host community members and internally displaced persons living in Al Makha City (Ta&#x2019;iz). IOM is finalizing the long awaited Al Makha City water supply scheme by installing the final 8.5 km of the transmission line, completing efforts of nearly two years to bring fresh water to individuals who are currently utilizing highly salinated water sources. The nine-month intervention includes the installation of the transmission line via a local contractor, construction of 5 inspection rooms to support monitoring and maintenance of the system, provision and installation of six borehole monitoring devices to measure water depletion, and installation of an automatic chlorine dosing system to ensure proper disinfection of the water supply. As part of efforts to ensure sustainability of the water supply scheme, IOM is providing capacity building to the Local Water and Sanitation Corporation on effective chlorination techniques. Further, community hygiene promoters are being trained to disseminate key messages on safe water practices and the responsible use of water resources.</narrative>
  </description>
